WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.086
Let's start with a question.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:05.150
Here is a county in Alabama.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.590
Greene County.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:11.820
Fewer than 8,000 people, spread across more than 600 square miles.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:12.771
Is it rural?

00:00:13.321 --> 00:00:16.571
The US Census Bureau says yes.

00:00:16.571 --> 00:00:20.258
Every resident lives in an area it classifies as rural.

00:00:20.821 --> 00:00:24.441
The federal list of metro areas says no.

00:00:24.441 --> 00:00:27.441
It counts Greene County as metropolitan,

00:00:27.441 --> 00:00:30.591
because of its commuting ties to a nearby city.

00:00:30.591 --> 00:00:31.741
Same county.

00:00:31.741 --> 00:00:33.501
Two official answers.

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:35.087
Neither one is a mistake.

00:00:35.654 --> 00:00:39.536
One public website even states both facts, one sentence apart.

00:00:40.086 --> 00:00:46.626
Now imagine combining health records, air quality, income, and hospital data,

00:00:46.626 --> 00:00:49.956
when we can't even agree on the word rural.

00:00:49.956 --> 00:00:55.226
That is what this tutorial is about: how to organize rural health data,

00:00:55.226 --> 00:00:56.646
and how to combine it.

00:00:57.211 --> 00:01:00.541
People have worked on rural health for more than thirty years,

00:01:00.541 --> 00:01:05.781
with serious money behind them, and the gaps are still there.

00:01:05.781 --> 00:01:09.011
That's why some people call it the hard problem.

00:01:09.011 --> 00:01:13.691
It's worth your time, because the people who live there deserve better, and

00:01:13.691 --> 00:01:17.831
because a solution that works in a rural place usually improves health care

00:01:17.831 --> 00:01:18.569
everywhere.

00:01:19.128 --> 00:01:21.988
You don't need any background to start.

00:01:21.988 --> 00:01:26.308
If you code, you'll see how joining two tables perfectly can still give a wrong

00:01:26.308 --> 00:01:27.688
answer.

00:01:27.688 --> 00:01:31.108
If you know health care, you'll see what the numbers leave out.

00:01:31.108 --> 00:01:32.708
If you think about business,

00:01:32.708 --> 00:01:37.098
you'll see how weak data can lead to a bad bet on a clinic.

00:01:37.098 --> 00:01:41.598
If you know communities, you'll see who the records never reach.

00:01:41.598 --> 00:01:45.998
And if you're simply curious, you'll learn to question any number.

00:01:45.998 --> 00:01:47.608
Good teams need all of you.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.470
In about 25 minutes, we'll cover four things.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.330
Why rural data behaves differently.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.650
Five questions to ask any dataset.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.020
Free tools that combine health and environmental data.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.775
And how to use AI for this work without getting fooled.

00:02:06.336 --> 00:02:12.116
Each part has a short video, a short reading, and a quick quiz.

00:02:12.116 --> 00:02:12.742
Let's begin.

